## What the WIP commit actually contained

`8945d07` had **only `.bunny-loop.log`** in it — none of the stage 2d work existed. So this commit is the whole stage.

## The change

Three lines of real code:

- `PANEL_IDS` gains `'07'…'10'`, so all ten preload together on "Nueva partida". Verified by watching the network rather than assuming: all ten requested, none failed.
- The `DEAL` table's `show` column becomes `level, 03, 07, 08, 04, 04, 09`.
- The theft moves its second narration to `10` and leaves Kael's line on `06`.

Text untouched — still all from `strings.js`, both languages.

Two things needed **no** change, which I checked rather than assumed: `isPanelId` is `/^[0-9]{2}$/`, which already matches `'10'`; and the CSS rule giving a real still a dark backdrop keys off `data-art^="0"` plus a `^="1"` exception for `'kael'`, so `10` lands on the dark field instead of one of the legacy colour gradients.

## Acceptance

`tools/story.mjs` — **10/10, exit 0, zero console errors**, and the three checks that name a panel still find them (04 on Kael's deal line, 06 on Kael's theft line), so the remap didn't strand those assertions.

Screenshots of 07, 08, 09, 10 in `.scratch/stage2d/`, all read by me and matching the brief's table:
- **07** — the elder as a hooded merchant, pack and staff, in a forest (ANCIANO)
- **08** — the bag of gold and jewels opened under a reaching gauntlet (ANCIANO)
- **09** — Kael from behind watching the lantern walk away (KAEL, "...Una noche.")
- **10** — the wide shot of the elder at the wall with the shadow behind him, **narration with no speaker name**, matching the brief's "—" on that line

`assets/` untouched (md5-verified, including the four new panels), and I re-ran `tools/reach.mjs` since `main.js` is shared — still passes (support 19/25/24, both pit deaths).

One note on my own process: my first capture pass missed panels 08 and 10 because I was pressing KeyZ twice per iteration, which advances two lines once a line is already complete — stepping straight over the panel I wanted. Fixed to one press per step; the game logic was never involved.
