Stage 2e: minion sprites from Nico's sheets

The three common enemies are drawn from the painted sheets instead of the
procedural silhouettes stage 1 left behind. assets/ stays read-only, so the
cut is committed to sprites/ and loaded the same way the cast is: a manifest
plus one PNG per enemy.

tools/cut_minions.py does the cutting. Magenta sheets get a chroma key, 1px
erosion and pink-fringe removal; rows and frames come from the horizontal and
vertical gaps. The spitter's loose fireball -- the one floating between cast
frames 3 and 4, not part of any frame -- is cut out as spitterShot.

Two things worth recording, both of which cost real time:

The crawler is a dark creature on a dark radial vignette, and the flood fill
the brief specifies does not work on it. Measured side by side on frame 1,
flood-filling from the box border at luma<28 leaves the soft brown vignette
behind as solid dark blobs -- exactly the "dark rectangle around the crawler"
the brief says to avoid -- while local contrast against a large box blur
leaves ~1 unit across the background and 20+ on the creature, an empty valley
between. Local contrast plus the largest connected component, a closing and a
hole fill, is what ships. The legs and back spikes all survive and no vignette
comes with them; .scratch/stage2e/crawler-compare.png is the comparison.

Resizing the cut is done on premultiplied alpha, in float, with a box filter.
Straight RGBA resize blends towards transparent black and haloes every
silhouette; a uint8 round trip after the premultiply quantises the colour to
0/1 across a soft edge and dividing back out by alpha multiplies that error
into saturated white. The first pass at this shipped solid white minions, which
is what the contact sheet caught. Cells are sized to twice the drawn size
(wisp 22, crawler 20, spitter 40 tall) rather than a fixed factor: the sheets
are 1300-2500px wide, so a constant reduction factor left the spitter atlas
16k px across and 18x its draw size.

Wiring: minions.js loads sprites/manifest.json and adapts the cut cells to the
drawn size; enemy.js maps wisp float/dash, crawler walk/lunge and spitter
idle/walk/cast, plays hurt for 0.15s and the real dead clip instead of the old
scale.y squash, and takes the 0.15s facing rule with facing the player while
attacking. Hitboxes follow the new sizes. The sprites are not tinted; the
per-level accent survives as the additive halo behind them, toned down to 0.22
for wisps because the painted art already carries its own light.

Two bugs found on the way. The spitter's onFire was optional-chained and never
assigned anywhere, so it had been holding position and shooting nothing at all;
it is wired now, and the fireball leaves on cast frame 3 so the projectile and
the animation agree. And the sprite outline shader offsets by one texel, which
suits a character cell drawn 1:1 but floods the interior of the 25x16 fireball
drawn at 12x8 -- every interior gap had four opaque neighbours, so the outline
filled them and the fireball rendered as a solid accent-coloured block. It gets
no outline.

Acceptance: tools/stage2e.mjs passes, exit 0, zero console errors, and
asserts the clip names, the drawn sizes, native facing, hitbox fit, that the
death plays the dead clip rather than squashing, and that the spitter puts a
textured flickering fireball in the world. Screenshots in .scratch/stage2e/,
all read: no magenta fringe anywhere, no dark rectangle around the crawler,
all three facing right, legs and spikes intact, the wisp's tendrils reading as
painted art rather than one green blob, and the death caught mid-dissolve.
tools/reach.mjs still passes, as do tools/story.mjs (10/10) and tools/smoke.mjs.

